      "problem": "Let $a=-28$ and $n=9.$ Find integers $q$ and $r$ such that $a=qn+r,$ where $0\\le r<n.$ Then, $q=$",
      "worked_solution": "Dividing $-28$ by $9$ using integer division, we notice that\n\n$$\n-4<-28÷9<-3.\n$$\n\nThe quotient $q$ is the left-hand value. So $q=-4.$\n\nNext, we find the remainder $r$ by solving $r=a-qn.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=-28-(-4)(9)=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write $-28=qn+r$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-28={{(-4)}_{⏟}}_{q}\\cdot 9+{{8}_{⏟}}_{r}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $q=-4.$",
